{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "For fixed particle_mass, the bound-state condition makes E_n depend on L and V0 through the standard finite square-well phase accumulation and penetration term, with a single constant mass controlling both terms.",
      "child_relation": "Replace the ideal abrupt square-well confinement by a physically smoother confining profile of the same nominal width and depth, so the boundary phase shift is no longer the square-well arcsine form and the E_n(L,V0,n) relation acquires regime-dependent curvature as states sample the softened edges.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "L",
        "V0",
        "n",
        "particle_mass"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "At fixed particle_mass and n, varying L or V0 near the shallow-well / near-threshold regime yields systematically different bending of E_n versus the confinement scale than the abrupt-wall model; low-lying deeply bound states approach the parent behavior, while near-threshold levels shift non-uniformly."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "eta",
        "expression": "particle_mass*V0*L**2/hbar**2",
        "role": "dimensionless confinement strength controlling how strongly finite-depth and edge-shape effects influence the spectrum"
      },
      {
        "name": "chi_n",
        "expression": "n*pi",
        "role": "level-phase scale used to compare geometric phase accumulation against the discrete index constraint"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when eta is order unity to moderate and the selected level is not deeply bound, so the wavefunction significantly samples the well edges; the contrast weakens for very deep or very wide wells where the interior dominates and the abrupt-wall approximation becomes accurate.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "Within training, moderate confinement already shows slight non-square-well curvature in E_n versus L and V0; in holdout, the same mechanism becomes clearer closer to the highest retained bound levels where edge sampling is stronger, without changing the basic bound-state setting.",
    "continuation_path": "In the limit of vanishing edge-smoothing length relative to L and to the interior wavelength, the child law reduces to the abrupt finite square-well quantization relation used by the parent.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The parent assumes an exactly piecewise-constant well with infinitely sharp interfaces, so all boundary effects are compressed into the square-well matching phase shift.",
    "baseline_assumption": "Finite confinement is represented by a piecewise constant potential region with abrupt boundaries.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Real heterostructure or electrostatic wells often have finite interface/field smoothing, which modifies the boundary phase shift and therefore the level quantization condition, especially for states with appreciable edge penetration.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "L",
      "V0",
      "n",
      "particle_mass",
      "hbar"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the abrupt-interface assumption rather than add a new process. The parent response maps L, V0, n, and particle_mass to E_n through one fixed square-well boundary-matching law. Hold particle_mass and n fixed and vary L or V0 across samples that remain bound. In the parent, one shared sharp-boundary phase-shift form governs all such conditions. In the refined model, finite edge smoothing changes that phase shift most strongly when particle_mass*V0*L**2/hbar**2 is not large and the selected state lies near the binding threshold, producing different curvature of E_n with respect to L and V0. This is a changed target mapping, not merely an auxiliary reinterpretation, because the predicted bound-level energy itself shifts as edge sampling changes. The effect is scientifically admissible in one-dimensional bound wells and does not require a new sampled input if one treats smoothing as an implicit correction to the idealized confinement assumption. Limitation: if all retained samples are deeply bound with large eta, the contrast may be weak and the parent may remain an excellent approximation."
  },
